@startuml 17_state_bus_controller
!include _style.iuml

title Bus controller lifecycle and error recovery (UML state machine)

[*] --> Uninitialised
Uninitialised --> CheckLines : init()
CheckLines : I2C: are SCL and SDA both high?
CheckLines : SPI: no check needed
CheckLines --> Idle : lines high / SPI
CheckLines --> Recovering : SDA or SCL low

Idle : bus free, controller configured
Idle --> InTransaction : lock acquired

InTransaction : messages / segments in progress
InTransaction --> Idle : end of transaction (STOP / CS released)
InTransaction --> Idle : AddrNack | DataNack\n/ STOP sent, bus healthy
InTransaction --> Aborting : transfer timeout | pin-low timeout\n| arbitration lost | FIFO error

Aborting : stop the peripheral, flush FIFOs,
Aborting : release lines (bit-bang)
Aborting --> Idle : I2C lines high | SPI
Aborting --> Recovering : I2C line still low

Recovering : hardware: hook.to_gpio()
Recovering : up to 9 SCL pulses until SDA high
Recovering : generate STOP
Recovering : hardware: hook.to_peripheral()
Recovering --> Idle : SDA and SCL high
Recovering --> Recovering : still low\n[attempt < cfg.recovery.max_attempts]
Recovering --> Faulted : attempts exhausted

Faulted : every call returns Error::BusStuck
Faulted : application decides: power-cycle target,
Faulted : reset board, degrade function
Faulted --> Recovering : reset_bus() by application

note right of Recovering
  9 clock pulses: a target that was interrupted in the
  middle of sending a byte holds SDA low; at most 8 data
  bits + 1 ACK clock let it finish and release SDA
  (bus clear procedure, NXP UM10204).
  Linux: recovery is done by the kernel driver, if supported.
  Zephyr: i2c_recover_bus(), if the driver implements it.
end note
@enduml
